{
  "headline": "Syrian woman killed in Houthi attack on King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh, Saudi Arabia",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Buthaina Ahmad Oweida, a 56-year-old Syrian mother of four, was among those killed during a Houthi attack on King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh, according to reporting by Arab News. Oweida, who was originally from the Idlib province of northern Syria, had lived in Saudi Arabia for four years after fleeing the civil war in her homeland. At the time of the attack, she was traveling from Riyadh to her home in Makkah after visiting her sister to assist in caring for an injured nephew. Her sister, Hayat Oweida, told Arab News from Idlib that Buthaina had intended to perform Umrah upon her return. Hayat stated that Buthaina had insisted on traveling despite family concerns regarding a previous attack on the airport. Hayat reported that a phone call between Buthaina and her nephew was suddenly cut off while she was at the airport."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Legal scholar questions NPP suit regarding presidential term limits in Ghana",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Legal scholar Kwaku Azar Asare has questioned whether a suit filed by the New Patriotic Party (NPP) at the Supreme Court of Ghana presents a justiciable constitutional dispute or constitutes a request for an advisory opinion. The NPP has filed the suit seeking an interpretation of Article 66(2) of the 1992 Constitution, which mandates a four-year presidential term and limits individuals to two terms in office. Specifically, the party is seeking a declaration that the two-term limit applies regardless of whether the terms are consecutive or non-consecutive. The legal action has drawn attention to the constitutional implications of presidential term limits ahead of the 2028 general election, though the filing does not establish that President John Dramani Mahama intends to seek another term."
    },
    {
      "text": "In a Facebook post, Mr. Asare argued that the current writ differs from previous constitutional cases pursued by the NPP, such as the 31st December Case and a challenge to police permit requirements for public demonstrations. He noted that those prior cases involved identifiable government actions or existing laws alleged to be inconsistent with the Constitution, whereas the current suit does not identify an allegedly unconstitutional enactment, act, or omission requiring judicial intervention. Mr. Asare stated that no one has filed to contest a third presidential election and no electoral authority has determined that a person elected twice is eligible for a third election. He maintained that a disagreement over the meaning of a constitutional provision does not, by itself, establish a justiciable controversy, suggesting the matter is at best a public debate over what the Constitution permits."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Africa CDC official warns Ebola outbreak could reach UK",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "Dr. Jean Kaseya, the director general of the Africa Centres for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), has warned that an unprecedented Ebola outbreak in Africa could reach the United Kingdom and other countries. Kaseya compared the potential spread of the illness to the Covid-19 pandemic, noting that the outbreak has already reached 10,000 cases and almost 5,000 deaths. He stated that countries are not safe unless they take appropriate measures and emphasized that more funding must be secured to increase preparedness and possible treatment to prevent the virus from spreading globally."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "headline": "Israeli election results remain uncertain as Ebola infections in Democratic Republic of Congo surpass 10,000",
  "pages": [
    {
      "text": "The election in Israel is currently too close to call following record voter turnout. Voters are deciding whether to retain Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu or replace him with an alternative, including his top rival, Gadi Eisenkot, a center-right former military chief of staff. Because final vote tallies may take time, if no bloc secures a majority, it could take days or weeks for party leaders to form a governing coalition and determine the next prime minister.  In the Democratic Republic of Congo, the Ebola outbreak has surpassed 10,000 people infected since it was declared in May, according to the latest government figures. The outbreak is described as the fastest-spreading in history and the largest ever in geographic scope, spanning from Nigeria to Kenya. Katie Thomas-Canfield, interim Ebola response director for the International Rescue Committee, stated that the speed at which the milestone was reached was surprising."
    },
    {
      "text": "The current Ebola outbreak has a death toll of nearly 5,000, which is second only to the West Africa outbreak of 2014-2016 that saw over 11,000 deaths. Approximately one-third of those who have died in this outbreak are children. Children under 5 are hit especially hard, with roughly three out of every four infected children dying. Mohamed Janabi, regional director for the World Health Organization Africa, noted at a press conference last week that the figures represent lives interrupted and communities seeking reassurance."
    }
  ]
}

